  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0030 database is a schema used for storing information related to push notifications. It contains five tables: auth_user, push_notifications_apnsdevice, push_notifications_gcmdevice, push_notifications_webpushdevice, and push_notifications_wnsdevice.\nThe auth_user table contains information about users who have registered for push notifications. Each row represents a single user, and the columns include an ID, password, last login, is superuser, username, first name, last name, email, is staff, is active, and date joined. The ID column is the primary key, and the other columns are not nullable.\nThe push_notifications_apnsdevice, push_notifications_gcmdevice, push_notifications_webpushdevice, and push_notifications_wnsdevice tables contain information about the devices that have registered for push notifications. Each row represents a single device, and the columns include an ID, name, active, date created, device ID, registration ID, user ID, application ID, and p256dh, auth, and browser columns. The ID column is the primary key, and the other columns are not nullable. The user ID column is a foreign key that references the ID column in the auth_user table.\nThe push_notifications_apnsdevice table contains information about Apple Push Notification Service (APNS) devices. The push_notifications_gcmdevice table contains information about Google Cloud Messaging (GCM) devices. The push_notifications_webpushdevice table contains information about web push devices. The push_notifications_wnsdevice table contains information about Windows Notification Service (WNS) devices.\nThe foreign key constraints ensure that the user ID column in the push_notifications_apnsdevice, push_notifications_gcmdevice, push_notifications_webpushdevice, and push_notifications_wnsdevice tables references the ID column in the auth_user table. This ensures that each device is associated with a specific user. The update and delete rules for the foreign key constraints are set to CASCADE, which means that if a user is deleted, all of their devices will also be deleted.\nIn summary, the schemabench_0030 database schema is used to store information about users and their devices for push notifications. It contains five tables, each with its own set of columns, and foreign key constraints ensure that the data is consistent and accurate.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing a mobile push notification system using Django. This system allows sending push notifications to mobile and web devices through multiple channels, including APNS (Apple Push Notification Service), FCM/GCM (Firebase Cloud Messaging/Google Cloud Messaging), WNS (Windows Notification Service), and WebPush. The system utilizes core Device models to manage and send notifications.\n\nObjectives\n\nThe primary objective is to enable efficient delivery of push notifications to a variety of devices. The system should facilitate sending both single and bulk notifications, handle device registrations, and update existing devices based on their registration IDs.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n- End Users: Individuals receiving notifications on their devices.\n- Administrators: Manage devices and notifications via the admin panel.\n- Developers: Implement and maintain the system within Django applications.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n- Device Models: Implement models for different notification services (GCMDevice, APNSDevice, WNSDevice, WebPushDevice) with key attributes like name, active status, user linkage, device ID, and registration ID.\n- Notification Sending: Support sending notifications through various services either to individual devices or in bulk.\n- Admin Panel: Provide an interface to manage devices and facilitate testing of notifications, including a feature to handle duplicate registration IDs by updating existing records.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Scalability: System must efficiently manage and send notifications to a potentially large number of devices.\n- Maintainability: Clean and adaptable code structure to allow easy updates and maintenance.\n- Performance: Ensure quick delivery of notifications with minimal delay for end users.\n- Security: Secure handling of user data and notifications, including proper management of user authentication and registration IDs.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_codellama13b/0030.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0030\nCREATE TABLE `auth_user` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_login` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_superuser` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `username` varchar(150) NOT NULL,\n  `first_name` varchar(30) NOT NULL,\n  `last_name` varchar(150) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(254) NOT NULL,\n  `is_staff` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `is_active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `date_joined` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `push_notifications_apnsdevice` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `device_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `registration_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `application_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `p256dh` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `auth` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `push_notifications_apnsdevice_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `push_notifications_gcmdevice` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `device_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `registration_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `application_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `p256dh` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `auth` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `push_notifications_gcmdevice_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `push_notifications_webpushdevice` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `device_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `registration_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `application_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `p256dh` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `auth` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `push_notifications_webpushdevice_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `push_notifications_wnsdevice` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `device_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `registration_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `application_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `p256dh` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `auth` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `push_notifications_wnsdevice_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
